  "note": "A2 liść · FIRST pack of the Oxford A2 vocab-gap build (VOCAB-BUILD-DIGEST batch 1). Grouping rationale: twelve Oxford-A2 job/role nouns pulled from the gap list as one theme, chosen because a1_inst_job already owns inst_identity — so every one of them lands in a frame the learner has had since A1 (Jestem nauczycielem → Jestem architektem) and the pack introduces ZERO structures. teaches_structures is [] by construction; the pack rides byc_present/inst_identity/poss_nom/present_uje/prep_w_loc/past_byc/miec_acc/ten_ta_to, all live long before this slot. A1/A2 already own nauczyciel, lekarz, kelner, kierowca, inżynier, rolnik, sprzedawca, menedżer, szef, naukowiec, pielęgniarka, artysta, aktor, piosenkarz, pisarz, tancerz, trener, dentysta — those are ANCHORS here, never re-taught. Judgment calls, all reported: (1) TITLE. 'Zawody' was rejected outright — a2_sport already teaches zawody as 'competition (sports event)', so a plural title would have read as 'Competitions 2'. The singular zawód (a2_work2, 'profession / trade') is unambiguous, hence 'Zawód 2'. (2) chef was DROPPED from the gap list without authoring: b1_people already teaches kucharz, glossed 'cook (m)' — the Oxford row survived only because the English gloss says 'cook', not 'chef'. Row deleted, logged in the digest. (3) INSTRUMENTAL FORMS are taught explicitly as whole Jestem/On jest phrases in block 2, not left to be derived, so every instrumental a sentence demands appears in a block and in teaches_lemmas (the every-form-taught rule). Nine are taught; the three nouns with no instrumental slot (złodziej, profesor, biznesmen) are used in the nominative only. (4) sportowiec → sportowcem drops the -e- of -ec. That alternation is a lexical fact about one word, taught with an explain, not a new system — the same shape as a2_countries' Anglik → Angielka → Angielką. (5) 'Muzyk gra w parku.' was drafted and then CUT: gra is taught twice over, as the verb (a1_present) and as the noun 'game' (a1_freetime), so the sentence would have been readable as 'A musician, a game in the park'. Replaced with 'Ten muzyk jest popularny.' (6) policja was FOLDED IN from codex/vocab/pl-frequency-crosscheck.tsv (rank 303), not from the Oxford gap list — it is thematically adjacent to zlodziej/zolnierz and is a plain noun needing no structure, so the cross-check's own instruction applied. It is the institution; b1_people teaches policjant (the person) later, and the two do not collide. Its explain names the singular/plural mismatch with English. (7) Adjectives and instrumental phrases take no gender badge — that field is for citation-form nouns only.",
